  "sections": [
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "heading": "What BMW Turbo & Ancillary Repair Involves",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "html": "Turbocharger failure or degraded performance affects boost, fuel economy, and engine response. Our repair process covers the complete turbo system - we don't just swap the core, we inspect, test, and replace every ancillary component that could cause repeat failure."
        },
        {
          "t": "ul",
          "items": [
            "**Turbo access and removal** - on BMW's **N63/S63 hot-vee** engines, turbos sit between cylinder banks, requiring 12-16 hours of skilled labour; other BMW engines place turbos at the side or front, which is significantly faster to access.",
            "**Full turbo inspection** - shaft play, blade damage, oil-seal condition, and wastegate actuator function.",
            "**Turbo rebuild or replacement** - new bearings, seals, and balancing (or a factory-spec replacement unit if the core is beyond repair).",
            "**Ancillary component replacement** - wastegate actuators, oil feed lines, coolant lines, gaskets, and sealing rings - these are replaced as a set to prevent future oil leaks or boost-control issues.",
            "**Reinstallation, bleeding, and testing** - the system is refitted, cooling circuits are bled, and we test for boost leaks, correct wastegate operation, and overall performance on the road."
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "html": "[Read more about our full engine inspection process →](/bmw-engine-inspection-assessment)"
        }
      ]
    },
