    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Frequently asked questions",
      "items": [
        {
          "q": "How do I know if my BMW turbo is failing?",
          "a": "Common signs include reduced power, excessive exhaust smoke, a whistling noise from the engine, or the engine management light coming on. We carry out a full inspection to confirm the exact cause."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can you repair a turbo or does it always need replacing?",
          "a": "It depends on the condition of the core. We inspect every turbo on removal - if the shaft, bearings, and housing are within spec, we can rebuild it. If the core is damaged, we recommend a replacement unit."
        },
        {
          "q": "What ancillary components do you replace as part of a turbo repair?",
          "a": "We replace wastegate actuators, oil feed lines, coolant lines, gaskets, and sealing rings as a complete set. This prevents repeat failures caused by degraded ancillaries."
        },
        {
          "q": "Why are BMW V8 turbos more expensive to repair?",
          "a": "On BMW's N63/S63 hot-vee V8 engines, the turbos are mounted between the cylinder banks. Access requires significant disassembly - typically 12-16 hours of labour compared to 4-6 hours on a 4- or 6-cylinder engine."
        },
        {
          "q": "How long does a BMW turbo repair take?",
          "a": "Most 4- and 6-cylinder turbo repairs are completed within 2-4 working days. V8 hot-vee repairs take longer due to the access complexity - typically 5-7 days."
        },
        {
          "q": "What warranty do you offer on turbo repairs?",
          "a": "All turbo repairs come with a 12-month unlimited-mileage warranty covering parts and labour for the work we carry out."
        }
      ]
    }
